the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5y(10-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=0.5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-119270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=0.5{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5-0.5{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y+0.5{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=5y-0.5{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=1,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2+y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=2-y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2+y)"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83848" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=y(2-y)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dt}=9y(3-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality is $k=9,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=-9{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27-9{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y+9{y}^{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119269" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dy}{dt}=27y-9{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A viral disease starts spreading through a community of $10\\,000$ people. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ infected with the virus is jointly proportional to the number of people $P(t)$ who have been infected at time $t,$ measured in days,  and the number who have not been infected. If the constant of proportionality is $0.25,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the growth rate of infected people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have been infected people at time $t,$ and the number who have not, the number of infected people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.25,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=10\\,000.$ Therefore,  the number of infected people is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.25P(10\\,000-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.25P(10\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.25P(10\\,000+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.25P(10\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=10\\,000P(0.25+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=10\\,000P(0.25-P)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.25P(10\\,000-P)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the population $P(t)$ in a certain city is jointly proportional to the population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the city's carrying capacity and the population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,8$ and the maximum possible population is $200\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the population, the population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,8,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=200\\,000.$ Therefore, the population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=200\\,000P(0.000\\,8-P)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,8P(200\\,000-P)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A population of $50\\,000\\,000$ people is being vaccinated against a disease. The rate of growth in the number of people $P(t)$ vaccinated is jointly proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ measured in months, and the number of unvaccinated people. If the constant of proportionality is $0.3,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the growth rate of vaccinated people is *jointly* proportional to the number of people who have been vaccinated at time $t,$ and the number of unvaccinated people, the number of vaccinated people follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.3,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000\\,000.$ Therefore, the number of people who have been vaccinated is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000\\,000P(0.3-P)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.3P(50\\,000\\,000-P)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the seal population $P(t)$ on a certain South African island is jointly proportional to the seal population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the island's carrying capacity and the seal population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,002$ and the maximum possible seal population is $50\\,000,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the seal population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the seal population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the seal population, the seal population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,002,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=50\\,000.$ Therefore, the seal population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=50\\,000P(0.000\\,002-P)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-83857" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,002P(50\\,000-P)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A pest infestation is spreading through a land with $300$ trees. The rate of growth in the number of trees $P(t)$ infected with the pest is jointly proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ measured in months,  and the number of uninfected trees. If the constant of proportionality is $0.2,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the growth rate of infected trees is *jointly* proportional to the number of trees that are infected at time $t,$ and the number of uninfected trees, the number of infected trees follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.2,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=300.$ Therefore, the number of trees that are infected is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0e02060f35e8c6fce74b4b0d637865e40d5f2ced6a3c7e20b58ea8c8247a99a3"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=-0.2P(300-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2+P)"] ["math" "\\frac{dP}{dt}=300P(0.2-P)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "q-119289" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.2P(300-P)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/state.json" "file_sha256" "8147e1b01229a646a7d8210d95b12d18cbb33829a3f6b2daa41787355f4708cc"} {"question" "e-7404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/example-7404.json" "file_sha256" "1829c48b454f6cb99e6162908a8c95015b4c709c188f4a27dce5890d083e0a74"} {"question" "e-7404" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y).\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/example-7404.json" "file_sha256" "1829c48b454f6cb99e6162908a8c95015b4c709c188f4a27dce5890d083e0a74"} {"question" "e-7321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120,$ which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/example-7321.json" "file_sha256" "64baf33cd2e68d1a82ad77f40b75da7f5d14902b7574310d6b3f84997b4ee13e"} {"question" "e-7321" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=120.$ Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14043005/example-7321.json" "file_sha256" "64baf33cd2e68d1a82ad77f40b75da7f5d14902b7574310d6b3f84997b4ee13e"}] "usage" {"e-7404" [] "e-7321" []} "basis" 1605 "retractions" [[17592186100690 :question/problem "Which of the following differential equations is a logistic growth equation?\n\n1. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56-7y^2$\n3. $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y^2-7$"] [17592186100690 :question/worked-solution "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFrom the given options, the only logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 56y-7y^2\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 7y(8-y)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the constant of proportionality $k=7$, and the carrying capacity is $a=8$."] [17592186100688 :question/problem "The growth rate of the hamster population $P(t)$ in a park is jointly proportional to the hamster population at time $t$, measured in years, and the difference between the park's carrying capacity and the hamster population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.005$ and the maximum possible hamster population is $120$, which differential equation could be used to model $P(t)$?"] [17592186100688 :question/worked-solution "Since the hamster population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the hamster population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the hamster population, the hamster population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = kP(a-P)\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a > P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.005$, and the carrying capacity is $a=120$. Therefore, the hamster population is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} P}{\\mathrm{d} t} = 0.005P(120-P)\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
